Theorem TF-3: Construct transfer produces FRESH(shape_from_ctor)

  Preconditions:
    - Instruction is `Construct { ty, ctor, args }` per Annex E §AIMS TF-3
    - `ctor` ranges over the constructor kinds enumerated in §3 TF-3 +
      Appendix A row 4: Struct / EnumVariant / ListLiteral / SetLiteral /
      MapLiteral / Tuple / Closure
    - FRESH(shape) = `(Owned, Linear, Once, Unique, BlockLocal, shape,
      {may_alloc=true})` per §3 TF-3 + Appendix A row 4

  Soundness property:
    Forall ctor in {Struct, EnumVariant, ListLiteral, SetLiteral, MapLiteral,
                    Tuple, Closure}.
      transfer_tf3(Construct { ctor, _ }) = FRESH(shape_from_ctor(ctor))
    AND L-6 layer (b) monotonicity holds: TF-3 depends ONLY on the static
    ctor token, not on input state; per-ctor f(_) = FRESH(shape) is constant.

  Proof obligation:
    Two-part discharge:
      (a) Shape mapping enumeration — for each ctor kind, the forward
          transfer rule maps to FRESH with the canonical shape per §3 TF-3:
            Struct -> ReusableCtor(Struct) [engine name: ReusableStruct]
            EnumVariant -> ReusableCtor(EnumVariant) [engine: ReusableEnum]
            ListLiteral -> CollectionBuffer
            SetLiteral -> CollectionBuffer
            MapLiteral -> CollectionBuffer
            Tuple -> NonReusable
            Closure -> NonReusable
          Other 6 per-dimension columns are constants: Owned / Linear / Once
          / Unique / BlockLocal / Effect={may_alloc=true} = 0b001.
      (b) L-6 layer (b) monotonicity — TF-3 is per-ctor constant: for fixed
          ctor token, f(_) = FRESH(shape). For ANY pair of input states
          (a, b) with a ≤ b, f(a) = f(b) = FRESH(shape), hence f(a) ≤ f(b)
          by L-4 reflexivity.

    Part (a) — ctor enumeration (7 kinds per §3 TF-3):
      Struct + EnumVariant: nominal type allocations, reusable shape lets
        downstream Reset/Reuse fire (DP-6 + RL-11).
      ListLiteral / SetLiteral / MapLiteral: collection allocations, all
        share the CollectionBuffer shape — distinct from ReusableCtor and
        from NonReusable per Annex E §AIMS Shape lattice.
      Tuple / Closure: NonReusable because tuples have no nominal type-id
        for reuse matching and closures carry environment-shape variance.
      All 7 ctors produce FRESH heap allocation (RC = 1, Unique). Effect =
      {may_alloc=true, may_share=false, may_throw=false} = 0b001 per §1.7.

    Part (b) — per-ctor constant-function monotonicity:
      For fixed ctor, TF-3 emits FRESH(shape_from_ctor(ctor)) — a constant
      function on its input state. Per L-4 reflexivity:
        FRESH(shape) ≤ FRESH(shape) holds (componentwise on the product
        lattice — every dimension reflexively comparable).
      Verified via product_le(fresh(shape), fresh(shape)) = Some(true) for
      each of the 4 distinct shapes emitted (ReusableStruct, ReusableEnum,
      CollectionBuffer, NonReusable).

    Engines dispatched:
      monotonicity (PRIMARY — L-6 layer (b) per-ctor constant-function check
        per section_04::verify_tf3_construct_fresh)
      case_analysis (PRIMARY — Appendix A row 4 forward-transfer enumeration
        across 7 ctor kinds per section_04::verify_tf3_construct_fresh)
      lattice (SECONDARY — accepts gracefully; product-order discharged §02)
      refinement (SECONDARY — accepts gracefully; TF-3 emits FRESH directly,
        not via refine())

  Expected:
    status: valid
    reason: section_04 monotonicity + case_analysis engines discharge TF-3 via 7-ctor enumeration with canonical shape mapping + per-ctor constant-function monotonicity check
